💡 **SUGGESTION: preserve the original error's stack when wrapping**

When a tail exists, `silentUpdateErrorWithTail` returns `new Error(base + '\n[silent-update stderr tail …]\n' + tail)` — a fresh error whose `stack` points here instead of at the original `cli_execute_command` throw, and which drops any extra properties (`code`, etc.) the original carried. Today's only caller logs the message, so this is cosmetic, but the whole point of gh-1074 is post-mortem debuggability; a wrapped error that loses the origin stack works against that.

```javascript
var wrapped = new Error(base + '\n[silent-update stderr tail …]\n' + tail);
if (e && e.stack) wrapped.stack = e.stack + '\nCaused by: wrapped with stderr tail (gh-1074)';
return wrapped;
```

(or copy enumerable props across). Fine to skip if callers never inspect beyond `.message`.

